/**
 * The constant a body that is generic over its scalar cannot write for itself.
 *
 * A numeric literal has a type, and inside `extend<Scalar: Numeric> Vector2<Scalar>` that type is `Scalar` - which no
 * back end fills in, so the literal stays an `Int` and a `Vector2<Float>` built from it would be wrong. Everything in
 * this package therefore derives the zero it needs from a value it already holds. The one is a member of `Real`
 * instead, because no operation a plain `Numeric` promises reaches it from a value at all.
 */

use Numeric from "std/number"

/**
 * The zero of the scalar type, derived from a value of it.
 *
 * A comparison against zero is what an emptiness test, a sign test and a guard against dividing by a zero length are
 * made of, and none of them can write `0`.
 *
 *     print zeroOf(2.5)
 *
 * # Open
 *
 * - The straight spelling is the literal `0`, and it is what this becomes once a back end substitutes the type of a
 *   literal into the instance it builds.
 * - `std/linear` holds the same function rather than this package importing it, and so would every other package that is
 *   generic over a scalar. The interpreter resolves no package import at all - it answers the names of `std/` out of
 *   its own natives - so a module of `std/` can only call what its own directory holds, and a shared home in
 *   `std/number` would make this package unrunnable there.
 */
public fn zeroOf<Scalar: Numeric>(value: Scalar): Scalar {
  value - value
}
